{"name":"Siren of Nature","hex":"#68a43c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"siren","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of a group of nymphs who lured mariners to their death on the rocks.","One who sings sweetly and charms."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a noise with, or as if with, a siren.","To drive with a siren on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/siren"},{"term":"nature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The way things are, the totality of all things in the physical universe and their order, especially the physical world in contrast to spiritual realms and flora and fauna as distinct from human conventions, art, and technology.","The particular way someone or something is, especially\nThe essential or innate characteristics of a person or thing which will always tend to manifest, especially in contrast to specific contexts, reason, religious duty, upbringing, and personal pretense or effort."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To endow with natural qualities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nature"}],"entries":[{"title":"Siren (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, sirens are female humanlike beings with alluring voices; they appear in a scene in the Odyssey in which Odysseus saves his crew's lives. Roman poets place them on some small islands called Sirenum Scopuli. In some later, rationalized traditions, the literal geography of the \"flowery\" island of Anthemoessa, or Anthemusa, is fixed: sometimes on Cape Pelorum and at others in the islands known as the Sirenuse, near Paestum, or in Capreae. All such locations were surrounded by cliffs and rocks. While some versions have depicted Sirens as woman-headed birds, other versions depict them as mermaids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/NAMA_Sir%C3%A8ne.jpg/330px-NAMA_Sir%C3%A8ne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siren_(mythology)"},{"title":"Siren (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Siren, known as Forbidden Siren in Europe and Australia, is a 2003 survival horror stealth video game developed and published by Sony Computer Entertainment for the PlayStation 2. It was originally released in Japan on November 6, 2003, and in other regions between March and April of the following year."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/78/Siren_art_box.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siren_(video_game)"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Greek_-_Vase_in_the_Form_of_a_Siren_-_Walters_482020.jpg/500px-Greek_-_Vase_in_the_Form_of_a_Siren_-_Walters_482020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Greek_-_Vase_in_the_Form_of_a_Siren_-_Walters_482020.jpg/1280px-Greek_-_Vase_in_the_Form_of_a_Siren_-_Walters_482020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Greek_-_Vase_in_the_Form_of_a_Siren_-_Walters_482020.jpg/500px-Greek_-_Vase_in_the_Form_of_a_Siren_-_Walters_482020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or